WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:03.000
- [Interviewer] All
right, Lehman, Lehman O.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.980
Where'd you grow up, where
are you from originally?

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:05.980
Where were you born?

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:09.280
- I'm from Hilton, Kentucky.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:10.780
- [Interviewer] That's
where you were born?

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:14.724
- Yeah, I was born up there road,

00:00:14.724 --> 00:00:16.070
about a half a mile out here,

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:18.670
I'll take you up there and show you.

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:21.003
That house is 225 years old.

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:23.725
That's where I was born,

00:00:23.725 --> 00:00:27.640
and the bed, the old bed is still up there

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:30.198
that me and my sister was born in,

00:00:30.198 --> 00:00:31.031
there's two of us left.

00:00:31.031 --> 00:00:32.910
- [Interviewer] And you're how old?

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:35.233
- I'm 90 years and 2 months.

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:38.660
Going on 92.
- That's great.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:41.160
- 91 I mean.
- You look great.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:43.080
So, tell me about your childhood,

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:44.730
you grew up with both your parents?

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:46.983
- Yeah, yeah.
- How was your childhood?

00:00:48.775 --> 00:00:53.775
- When my father, I was five
year old when my dad died.

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:57.420
- Oh, I'm sorry.
- Five years old, yeah.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.110
I do remember a time or two,

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:03.383
him taking me to the store down here

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:07.693
and buy me some candy, I've had.

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:13.900
I missed him pretty bad, but my father

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:15.780
was in the Spanish-American War

00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:19.863
and we got pictures of him up there.

00:01:20.764 --> 00:01:21.700
I'll take you all up there

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:23.490
and show you this house where I was born,

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:25.780
they got pictures of him up there.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:27.200
- [Interviewer] How did
you lose your father?

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.020
- My father, back then they
didn't have much hospital,

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:34.003
he died in the Red Bird Hospital,

00:01:35.342 --> 00:01:39.877
died with some kind of cancer,

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:44.230
I don't know what killed him really.

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:46.403
They didn't have much doctors then.

00:01:48.706 --> 00:01:50.750
But we had to take him to Red Bird,

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:53.650
it's about from here to Red Bird,

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:55.493
had to carry him on stretchers.

00:01:57.362 --> 00:02:00.070
And they was five men
carried him over there,

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:02.320
he lived in the hospital over there

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:06.700
about a week and died over there

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:08.320
and they had to go and carry him back

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:09.793
we didn't have no vehicles.

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:12.800
Had to carry him back on stretchers

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:17.690
and they buried him down here
at the Hilton post office

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:19.220
down here about a mile.

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:20.610
- [Interviewer] So tell me about

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:22.580
how you grew up, did you have electricity?

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:23.413
You didn't have water?

00:02:23.413 --> 00:02:24.750
You didn't have anything?

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:28.860
- Well, I grew up, we
didn't have no bathroom

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:32.383
in the house, we had a toilet, like that,

00:02:32.383 --> 00:02:35.690
just a regular toilet outside the house.

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:37.990
And of course we didn't
have any water in the house,

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:42.160
so we had that big well up there,

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:46.820
after the interview, I'll take
you up there and show you.

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:49.800
Had to draw water, had a rope

00:02:51.284 --> 00:02:55.450
and a pulley and it draw
the water out of the well,

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:59.300
and back then, you raised most of the food

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:01.820
that you eat in the summertime.

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:05.953
And then we planned on
doing through winter,

00:03:05.953 --> 00:03:09.533
cabbage and potatoes,
and something like that.

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:15.960
But no, I told you, you think before

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:21.768
that I worked for my uncle up there,

00:03:21.768 --> 00:03:24.710
he worked me very, very hard

00:03:26.052 --> 00:03:30.723
for 25 cents from 6
o'clock to six at night.

00:03:32.251 --> 00:03:37.251
And one time, we took a load of timbers

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:40.703
to a mine over there in Harlan, Kentucky.

00:03:42.149 --> 00:03:45.962
And I felt sure that he had kids,

00:03:45.962 --> 00:03:49.220
he draw a pension, he had money,

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:54.220
but he wouldn't even
buy me a 10 cent hotdog

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:56.863
and a nickel pop.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:01.010
I had to sit there and
they ate a big dinner,

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:05.676
him and his son, and I had to sit there

00:04:05.676 --> 00:04:08.783
and just drink water.

00:04:10.064 --> 00:04:11.430
It was hard for me to believe

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:15.530
that he wouldn't spend 15 cents,

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:20.313
and I told him if he
would buy me a hotdog,

00:04:22.215 --> 00:04:25.303
and a small pop back
then was just a nickel.

00:04:26.819 --> 00:04:29.010
He said, you don't get paid 'til.

00:04:30.236 --> 00:04:31.743
He wouldn't do it, anyway.

00:04:34.733 --> 00:04:38.010
After that, we came back home,

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:39.370
I told my mother about that,

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:41.093
and she said, son I can't believe,

00:04:42.399 --> 00:04:43.793
that with him drawing a pension,

00:04:44.716 --> 00:04:49.716
that he wouldn't buy you a 10
cent hotdog and a nickel pop.

00:04:52.736 --> 00:04:55.710
But you know, I decided, I told mother,

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:01.803
I decided I wanted to go for the Army,

00:05:02.856 --> 00:05:07.597
but anyway, she said son,
I have to tell them I'm 17,

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:12.663
and you have to back it up
to a recruiting officer,

00:05:14.158 --> 00:05:16.780
and she said, I don't want you to go,

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:20.100
but I didn't, I was told I
was about to go and try it,

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:22.270
we didn't have any vehicle back then,

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:25.190
we had to go down here and Ed Wilson,

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:28.260
a cousin of mine, drove
a black brother in a bus,

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:30.473
we got on that black brother's bus,

00:05:31.937 --> 00:05:35.254
and went to Harlan, she went with me,

00:05:35.254 --> 00:05:36.700
she decided she would do that.

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:39.340
I got over there to
that recruiting officer,

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:42.156
and I told him I was 17 years old.

00:05:42.156 --> 00:05:43.439
- [Interviewer] How old were you actually?

00:05:43.439 --> 00:05:45.494
- Huh?
- How old were you actually?

00:05:45.494 --> 00:05:48.797
- 13, I was 13, but you had to be 17

00:05:48.797 --> 00:05:52.210
before you could get in
the United States Army.

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:54.930
And that recruiting officer said,

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:59.880
Mr. Wilson, you look very, very, young,

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:02.563
he said, I don't believe
you are 17 years old.

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:06.303
I said, yes my mother's out
there in the waiting room,

00:06:07.217 --> 00:06:11.233
and you go out there and
ask her how old I am.

00:06:12.157 --> 00:06:14.840
I just told her what
to tell him, you know?

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:17.143
And she said, he asked her,

00:06:19.036 --> 00:06:23.200
and she said he's 17, and he said,

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:30.470
well I'll tell you what, you go come here

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:32.180
in the morning, Mr. Wilson.

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:34.770
We had to ride the bus,
like I said before.

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:39.770
And I'll have you a ticket made
to Camp Atterbury, Indiana.

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:46.260
And you go up there
and have an examination

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:49.343
to see if he qualifies for the Army.

00:06:49.343 --> 00:06:52.560
Well I went over the next
morning and called a bus,

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:55.680
I had never been anywhere
out of the State of Kentucky.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:59.203
But anyway, I went to
Camp Atterbury, Indiana,

00:06:59.203 --> 00:07:02.140
and I started to train
when I went up there.

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:06.300
I, when I got up there,
I didn't have much money,

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:11.300
I had $25 or $30 was all the money I had.

00:07:12.670 --> 00:07:17.670
But anyway, I went
through a taxi cab outfit,

00:07:18.850 --> 00:07:21.653
and I didn't know where that place was.

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:24.940
He drove me 100 miles

00:07:24.940 --> 00:07:28.080
and took about all the money that I had,

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:33.080
and I went in there and to be examined,

00:07:34.964 --> 00:07:39.964
and I was examined and this
recruiting officer said to me

00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:46.284
that I didn't look like
I was 17, but anyway,

00:07:46.284 --> 00:07:51.284
he said we need men, you say you're 17,

00:07:52.222 --> 00:07:54.672
and your mother said you
was, I'll just pass you.

00:07:56.140 --> 00:07:59.760
And I tell you, they passed me

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:03.970
and he sent me to Camp Atterbury, Indiana,

00:08:03.970 --> 00:08:06.490
like I said and I was examined

00:08:06.490 --> 00:08:08.780
and I was 10 pound underweight,

00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:10.110
but they passed me anyway,

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:14.360
so I went from Camp Atterbury, Indiana

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:17.910
to Fort Knox, Kentucky for basic training.

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:20.240
I was in basic training in Ft. Knox

00:08:21.957 --> 00:08:23.810
for three months,

00:08:23.810 --> 00:08:28.810
and I came home on leave, and for 14 days,

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:34.093
and they sent me to the South Pacific,

00:08:38.436 --> 00:08:43.436
100 miles out of, I forget what
the name of that place was.

00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:48.150
And I was up there for two years.

00:08:48.150 --> 00:08:51.867
I stayed two years, I had lived so hard,

00:08:51.867 --> 00:08:54.393
I came back and got a job in the mines,

00:08:56.245 --> 00:08:58.690
and went to work.

00:08:58.690 --> 00:09:00.915
- [Interviewer] How many
years of school did you get?

00:09:00.915 --> 00:09:02.516
- Huh?
- How many years of school

00:09:02.516 --> 00:09:03.530
did you get?
- 8th grade.

00:09:03.530 --> 00:09:06.503
- 8th grade.
- Well, what is was,

00:09:06.503 --> 00:09:09.680
I started high school, but
anyway I went over there,

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:14.680
and these young fellas, I
was about 18 or 20 years old,

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:16.680
there was three of them.

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:21.680
They got jealous over me over
a girl that went to school

00:09:21.890 --> 00:09:26.802
and they broke to door down
and come into the school

00:09:26.802 --> 00:09:28.202
and almost beat me to death.

00:09:29.502 --> 00:09:31.140
The principal was downstairs,
he happened to hear it,

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:32.490
or they would've killed me.

00:09:34.907 --> 00:09:38.220
And but anyway, he got them off of me,

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:42.470
and I left the next morning
from school over there

00:09:42.470 --> 00:09:47.470
at Red Bird, I didn't
go, I went to the Army.

00:09:49.655 --> 00:09:50.660
- [Interviewer] And then eventually,

00:09:50.660 --> 00:09:53.083
after the Army, and you
worked as a coal miner?

00:09:53.083 --> 00:09:55.383
- Yeah, I worked in the
coal mines then, yeah.

00:09:57.560 --> 00:09:59.040
- [Interviewer] And
growing up in Appalachia,

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:02.340
what's would've been
the most difficult parts

00:10:02.340 --> 00:10:04.100
of living in Appalachia?

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:06.190
- Well, I'll tell you what,

00:10:06.190 --> 00:10:09.603
back then we didn't have no juice,

00:10:11.960 --> 00:10:13.403
had to use the lamp light,

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:17.673
and of course didn't have
no water in the house.

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:21.850
And back when we was raised up,

00:10:21.850 --> 00:10:25.880
it was pretty, it was hard, I'll tell you,

00:10:25.880 --> 00:10:27.320
and no road then in here,

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:29.223
the road ran up by this creek here.

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:33.353
It was a long time before
they built the road up there.

00:10:35.730 --> 00:10:38.590
But our road came out this creek up there,

00:10:38.590 --> 00:10:41.393
where I was born and raised
at this house out here.

00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:46.970
But we didn't have no
road, didn't have much of,

00:10:46.970 --> 00:10:48.729
you walked everywhere you went.

00:10:48.729 --> 00:10:49.562
- [Interviewer] There were
go vehicles back then,

00:10:49.562 --> 00:10:50.630
there were no vehicles back then.

00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:51.463
- Nope, nope.

00:10:51.463 --> 00:10:52.603
- [Interviewer] How'd you get around?

00:10:54.005 --> 00:10:54.855
- We rode a mule.

00:11:00.030 --> 00:11:03.990
My dad had some fine team of mules

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:06.620
and he'd worked them, and hauled them,

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:10.079
and that's how he got around,

00:11:10.079 --> 00:11:11.553
he rode the mule.

00:11:11.553 --> 00:11:12.671
- [Interviewer] When you were a young man,

00:11:12.671 --> 00:11:15.180
did you have dreams of doing
something with your life?

00:11:17.818 --> 00:11:20.354
- When I was a young man,
I thought I might try

00:11:20.354 --> 00:11:23.739
to retire in the Army, I was young,

00:11:23.739 --> 00:11:26.572
I was 13 but I told them I was 17.

00:11:29.210 --> 00:11:32.060
But I stayed two years
and I decided I'd get out,

00:11:32.060 --> 00:11:33.823
and I got out and got a job.

00:11:34.982 --> 00:11:38.970
And then of course I've
been very tired a long time.

00:11:38.970 --> 00:11:41.310
- [Interviewer] Have you
ever considered leaving

00:11:41.310 --> 00:11:42.570
this part of the country?

00:11:42.570 --> 00:11:44.374
Moving somewhere else?

00:11:44.374 --> 00:11:45.207
- Well, you know, by golly,

00:11:46.550 --> 00:11:49.023
I got one sister left, she lives in Ohio,

00:11:49.915 --> 00:11:52.013
wanted me to come up there,

00:11:52.954 --> 00:11:55.738
but I've been born and raised here on

00:11:55.738 --> 00:11:59.900
this, they call this Big
Branch, this holler here.

00:12:00.898 --> 00:12:02.033
I will stay here.

00:12:03.836 --> 00:12:04.840
- [Interviewer] You're happy here.

00:12:04.840 --> 00:12:05.703
- Yeah, right.

00:12:06.970 --> 00:12:08.653
You've been here that many years,

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:13.533
like I told you I'm 90
years old in about 2 months.

00:12:15.060 --> 00:12:16.560
Too old to leave now (laughs).

00:12:20.438 --> 00:12:21.271
- [Interviewer] What
are your best memories?

00:12:21.271 --> 00:12:22.597
- Huh?

00:12:22.597 --> 00:12:23.810
- [Interviewer] What
are you best memories?

00:12:23.810 --> 00:12:25.110
- Well I'll tell you what,

00:12:26.027 --> 00:12:28.863
back when I, best memories I ever had,

00:12:30.936 --> 00:12:33.330
up there where I was
born and raised up here

00:12:33.330 --> 00:12:35.760
at this old house, I
wanna take you up there

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:37.500
and show you that before you leave,

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:40.000
I'd like for you to see, 225 years old.

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:42.640
I guess that's the best
memories I can think of here.

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:43.550
- [Interviewer] I'd love to see it.

00:12:43.550 --> 00:12:44.383
- Yeah.

00:12:45.570 --> 00:12:49.170
What has been the most important
technological advancement

00:12:49.170 --> 00:12:50.720
that has happened in your life?

00:12:51.568 --> 00:12:54.623
- [Lehman] Well, automobile, back when,

00:12:55.650 --> 00:12:57.913
I didn't really have nothing,

00:12:57.913 --> 00:12:59.434
I rode a mule all the time,

00:12:59.434 --> 00:13:03.780
I didn't really have no vehicle
back when I was growing up.

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:06.680
- [Interviewer] How would you
visit a doctor or a dentist?

00:13:08.330 --> 00:13:13.330
- [Lehman] Well, back then
I'll tell you one time we went.

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:16.860
Me and my sister, there was two of us,

00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:18.533
we both got pneumonia.

00:13:19.550 --> 00:13:23.470
Mack Wilson down here at
the Memphis Holler, here.

00:13:23.470 --> 00:13:27.963
He had an old A Model car,

00:13:29.826 --> 00:13:33.910
and mother called him and he came up here

00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:37.010
at this old place up here, 225 years old.

00:13:37.010 --> 00:13:40.770
He came up there and
took us to the hospital

00:13:40.770 --> 00:13:43.473
over there in Harlan, Kentucky.

00:13:44.721 --> 00:13:47.577
We most stayed in there
for about seven, eight days

00:13:48.778 --> 00:13:50.363
with pneumonia, and got out.

00:13:55.950 --> 00:13:57.060
- [Interviewer] And what's
the most important lesson

00:13:57.060 --> 00:13:58.660
you've learned in your 90 years?

00:14:07.670 --> 00:14:10.687
- Well you know I just ain't
the old run of the bull,

00:14:10.687 --> 00:14:13.530
but I can see I'll do pretty good,

00:14:13.530 --> 00:14:16.440
to be 90 years and 2 months old.

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:19.270
I can still drive and stuff.

00:14:19.270 --> 00:14:20.560
- [Interviewer] You mow the lawn still?

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:21.393
- Yeah.

00:14:21.393 --> 00:14:22.610
I can drive pretty good.

00:14:22.610 --> 00:14:24.760
I'll tell you this one story here,

00:14:24.760 --> 00:14:28.460
you know, about six months ago,

00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:32.090
I was going to Harlan over there

00:14:34.590 --> 00:14:38.650
and I got stopped on Pine
Mountain, by the state police.

00:14:38.650 --> 00:14:40.993
He just joined, this was on a Friday.

00:14:41.932 --> 00:14:46.932
And he just joined, he caught me up there,

00:14:46.997 --> 00:14:50.570
and of course I had
insurance and everything

00:14:50.570 --> 00:14:54.699
and he asked me, he looked
at my driver's license,

00:14:54.699 --> 00:14:57.770
and he said, buddy, you too
old to drive, aren't you?

00:14:57.770 --> 00:15:00.107
- I said, buddy you can't
get anybody to drive you

00:15:00.107 --> 00:15:01.683
any time you want to.

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:05.030
- He said, I want you to come in,

00:15:05.030 --> 00:15:07.550
that was on a Friday.

00:15:07.550 --> 00:15:10.140
He said, I want you to
come in Monday morning

00:15:10.140 --> 00:15:12.203
and give your driver's license up.

00:15:13.067 --> 00:15:14.800
And I said, buddy, you can't get anybody

00:15:14.800 --> 00:15:16.650
to drive you, you know.

00:15:16.650 --> 00:15:18.490
And he said I want you
to come in Monday morning

00:15:18.490 --> 00:15:20.360
to give your driver's license up.

00:15:20.360 --> 00:15:22.660
He says, do you have anybody

00:15:23.740 --> 00:15:28.120
that I could talk to, a
daughter or something?

00:15:28.120 --> 00:15:30.640
And I got a daughter that's a lawyer

00:15:30.640 --> 00:15:32.840
for the University of Kentucky, yeah.

00:15:32.840 --> 00:15:36.520
I give her my daughter's
number, I give him.

00:15:36.520 --> 00:15:39.820
But anyway, he called her,

00:15:39.820 --> 00:15:43.360
he said I got your dad
stopped here on Pine Mountain.

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:46.830
And of course, he's got
insurance and everything,

00:15:46.830 --> 00:15:49.483
but I'm asking because
he's too old to drive.

00:15:50.370 --> 00:15:52.740
And she said to him, she said,

00:15:52.740 --> 00:15:54.490
let me tell you something or other.

00:15:56.490 --> 00:15:58.820
What have you got him stopped for?

00:15:58.820 --> 00:16:01.090
He said an inch on the yellow line.

00:16:01.090 --> 00:16:04.600
She told him, I could hear her,

00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:06.460
she said let me tell you
something or another,

00:16:06.460 --> 00:16:09.990
she said I'm a lawyer for
the University of Kentucky,

00:16:09.990 --> 00:16:14.590
my son's a lawyer, and my
husband is circuit judge.

00:16:14.590 --> 00:16:17.460
And she said, we'll be
there Monday morning

00:16:17.460 --> 00:16:20.380
at 9 o'clock, and we'll see,

00:16:20.380 --> 00:16:22.083
she said that's not even a fine.

00:16:24.976 --> 00:16:27.790
And of course he hung up,

00:16:27.790 --> 00:16:31.860
and he told me, you know, after
he got done talking to her,

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:34.920
he said, I'll tell you what
I'm gonna do, Mr. Wilson,

00:16:34.920 --> 00:16:39.920
I'm gonna follow you to
the foot of Pine Mountain,

00:16:40.480 --> 00:16:43.240
and your daughter says
that's not even a fine,

00:16:43.240 --> 00:16:45.330
but I think it is.

00:16:45.330 --> 00:16:50.057
Well I said, I don't know
nothing, I don't know.

00:16:50.057 --> 00:16:51.770
And I said well you can follow me

00:16:51.770 --> 00:16:53.760
to the foot of Pine Mountain.

00:16:53.760 --> 00:16:56.026
And he followed me to the
foot of Pine Mountain,

00:16:56.026 --> 00:16:57.888
and he throw them blue
lights back on me again,

00:16:57.888 --> 00:17:00.483
and he came out there and said to me,

00:17:01.578 --> 00:17:05.163
he said Mr. Wilson, I'm sorry
I done you the way I done you.

00:17:06.528 --> 00:17:07.830
He said, you don't have to
come in and give your license,

00:17:07.830 --> 00:17:10.300
and that was the end of it.

00:17:12.360 --> 00:17:13.760
- [Interviewer] All right, Lehman,

00:17:13.760 --> 00:17:15.200
well thank you so much for talking with me

00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:17.410
and sharing your interesting story.

00:17:17.410 --> 00:17:20.500
- Yeah, buddy, thank
you for interviewing me,

00:17:20.500 --> 00:17:21.333
I appreciate that.

00:17:21.333 --> 00:17:23.239
- [Interviewer] Yeah,
you've had a great life.

00:17:23.239 --> 00:17:24.270
- Yeah.
- Good luck.

00:17:24.270 --> 00:17:26.484
- [Lehman] Good luck,
I appreciate that man,

00:17:26.484 --> 00:17:28.346
thank you.
- Thank you.

00:17:28.346 --> 00:17:31.013
- [Lehman] Thank you, thank you.

